.\" perror.3 - print error message
.TH PERROR 3 "2026-05-30" "Substrate" "Library Functions Manual"
.SH NAME
perror \- print error message to standard error
.SH LIBRARY
.B libc
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <stdio.h>
.PP
.BI "void perror(const char *" s ");"
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.BR perror ()
function writes a message describing the current value of the global variable
.I errno
to the standard error stream
.RB ( stderr ).
.PP
If
.I s
is non-NULL and points to a non-empty string, the output has the form:
.PP
.RS 4
.IB s ": error " errno
.RE
.PP
followed by a newline.
Otherwise (if
.I s
is NULL or points to an empty string), the output is:
.PP
.RS 4
.BI "error " errno
.RE
.PP
followed by a newline.
.PP
.B NOTE:
The Substrate
.BR perror ()
implementation does
.I not
translate
.I errno
into a textual diagnostic message.
The numeric value of
.I errno
is printed in decimal as the literal token following
.BR error .
This differs from the behavior mandated by ISO C and POSIX, which require a human-readable message equivalent to that returned by
.BR strerror (3).
Programs requiring a textual error description should call
.BR strerror (3)
directly.
.PP
The value of
.I errno
is read at the time
.BR perror ()
is called; it is the caller's responsibility to invoke
.BR perror ()
before any intervening library call has the opportunity to modify
.IR errno .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR strerror (3),
.BR fprintf (3),
.BR errno (3)
.SH STANDARDS
The
.BR perror ()
function conforms to ISO C99 and IEEE Std 1003.1-2008 (POSIX.1) in interface, but the Substrate implementation deviates by emitting the decimal
.I errno
value rather than a translated diagnostic string.
.SH EXAMPLES
The following program reports a failed open using
.BR perror ().
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
#include <stdio.h>

int
main(void)
{
    FILE *fp = fopen("/nonexistent", "r");

    if (fp == NULL) {
        perror("/nonexistent");
        return 1;
    }
    fclose(fp);
    return 0;
}
.EE
.RE
